  "hints": [
    {
      "tier": 1,
      "text": "You do not need to read Tamil letter by letter to start. Count the words (the spaces) in each Tamil name and compare with the English list: how many English names are one word, two words, three words? That alone splits the 25 items into small groups.",
      "xpCost": 2
    },
    {
      "tier": 2,
      "text": "Tamil writing is an abugida: each consonant sign carries a built-in vowel, an extra stroke changes that vowel, and a dot above the consonant cancels the vowel altogether. So a repeated English word or syllable shows up as exactly the same run of Tamil signs. Look for names that share a word (two of them begin with the same first word) and for a syllable that turns up at the start of one English name and in the middle of another.",
      "xpCost": 3
    },
    {
      "tier": 3,
      "text": "Practise on a name that is not in the list: Singapore is written சிங்கப்பூர். Reading left to right, சி is si, ங் is ng (the dot cancels its vowel), க is ka, ப் is p, பூ is puu and ர் is r, giving si-ng-ka-p-puu-r. Notice how ங் spells the English '-ng' and how a dotted consonant means 'this letter with no vowel after it'. Apply the same slicing to the station names, and remember that a couple of the names may be translated rather than spelled out.",
      "xpCost": 4
    }
  ],
  "explanationHtml": "<h3>What kind of writing system is this?</h3><p>Tamil is read left to right, like English. It is an <em>abugida</em>: every consonant letter already contains a vowel (usually a short 'a'), extra strokes change that vowel, and a dot written above a consonant means 'no vowel here'. So <span class=\"data\">க</span> is <em>ka</em>, <span class=\"data\">கா</span> is <em>kaa</em>, and <span class=\"data\">க்</span> is just <em>k</em>. This matters because it means a syllable of English always turns into the same little cluster of Tamil signs, so repeated words in the station list are easy to spot even if you cannot read the script.</p><h3>Step 1: count the words</h3><p>Look at the spaces. Only three English names have three words: Choa Chu Kang, Yio Chu Kang and Ang Mo Kio. Only three Tamil entries have three words: E2, E6 and E11.</p><p>Two of the English three-word names end with the same two words, 'Chu Kang'. In Tamil, E6 and E11 both end with <span class=\"data\">சூ காங்</span>, while E2 ends with something different. That pins E2 down as Ang Mo Kio. Check it: <span class=\"data\">அங்</span> = <em>ang</em> (the dot on <span class=\"data\">ங்</span> makes it a bare '-ng'), <span class=\"data\">மோ</span> = <em>mo</em>, <span class=\"data\">கியோ</span> = <em>kio</em>.</p><p>Now separate the two Chu Kang stations by their first word. E11 starts <span class=\"data\">சுவா</span> (<em>chu-vaa</em>, the Tamil spelling of Choa) and E6 starts <span class=\"data\">இயோ</span> (<em>i-yo</em>, Yio). Tamil words do not begin with 'y' plus a vowel very comfortably, so 'Yio' picks up a supporting <span class=\"data\">இ</span> at the front. The <span class=\"data\">ச</span>-letter, which spells the 'ch/c' sound, is exactly the clue the official solution points to.</p><h3>Step 2: the repeated word 'Bukit'</h3><p>Two English names begin with 'Bukit': Bukit Batok and Bukit Gombak. Two Tamil names begin with the same word, <span class=\"data\">புக்கிட்</span> — E20 and E21. Which second word is which? The syllable 'ba' is written <span class=\"data\">பா</span>. In E21 the second word <span class=\"data\">பாத்தோக்</span> <em>starts</em> with <span class=\"data\">பா</span>, matching Ba-tok. In E20 the second word <span class=\"data\">கொம்பாக்</span> has <span class=\"data\">பா</span> in the <em>middle</em>, matching Gom-bak. So E21 is Bukit Batok and E20 is Bukit Gombak.</p><h3>Step 3: work through the single-word names</h3><p>For the rest, slice each Tamil word into syllables and compare shape and length with the English names:</p><ul><li><span class=\"data\">அட்மிரல்டி</span> = ad-mi-ral-di, Admiralty (E3)</li><li><span class=\"data\">ஆர்ச்சர்ட்</span> = aar-char-t, Orchard (E4)</li><li><span class=\"data\">உட்லண்ட்ஸ்</span> = ud-land-s, Woodlands (E7)</li><li><span class=\"data\">காதிப்</span> = kaa-thi-p, Khatib (E8)</li><li><span class=\"data\">கிராஞ்சி</span> = ki-raan-ji, Kranji (E9)</li><li><span class=\"data\">சாமர்செட்</span> = saa-mar-se-t, Somerset (E10)</li><li><span class=\"data\">செம்பாவாங்</span> = sem-baa-vaang, Sembawang (E12)</li><li><span class=\"data\">நியூட்டன்</span> = ni-yuu-t-tan, Newton (E16)</li><li><span class=\"data\">நொவீனா</span> = no-vii-naa, Novena (E17)</li><li><span class=\"data\">பிரேடல்</span> = pi-re-dal, Braddell (E18)</li><li><span class=\"data\">மார்சிலிங்</span> = maar-si-ling, Marsling (E23)</li></ul><p>Watch the tricky near-pair: E19 <span class=\"data\">பீஷான்</span> and E24 <span class=\"data\">யீஷூன்</span> both contain <span class=\"data\">ஷ</span> (the 'sh' sound), but the first letter differs — <span class=\"data\">பீ</span> is <em>bii</em> and <span class=\"data\">யீ</span> is <em>yii</em>. That is Bishan versus Yishun.</p><h3>Step 4: two-word names</h3><p>E13 <span class=\"data\">டோபி காட்</span> is do-bi kaa-t, Dhoby Ghaut. E14 <span class=\"data\">தோ பாயோ</span> is tho paa-yo, Toa Payoh. E22 <span class=\"data\">மரீனா பே</span> is ma-rii-naa pe, Marina Bay. E25 <span class=\"data\">ராஃபிள்ஸ் பிளேஸ்</span> is Raffles Place — the three-dot sign <span class=\"data\">ஃ</span> (called <em>aaytam</em>) is used in modern Tamil to write foreign sounds like 'f'. E5 <span class=\"data\">இயூ டீ</span> is i-yuu dii, Yew Tee, with the same supporting <span class=\"data\">இ</span> we saw in Yio.</p><h3>Step 5: the names that are translated, not spelled out</h3><p>Two entries do not sound like their English names at all, because SMRT translated part or all of them.</p><p>E1 <span class=\"data\">ஜூரோங் கிழக்கு</span>: the first word is <em>juurong</em>, clearly Jurong, but <span class=\"data\">கிழக்கு</span> (<em>kizhakku</em>) is the Tamil word for 'east'. So E1 is Jurong East.</p><p>E15 <span class=\"data\">நகர மண்டபம்</span> (<em>nagara mandapam</em>) contains no English sounds at all: it literally means 'city hall'. Even without knowing the words, once everything else is matched only City Hall is left, so elimination gives it to you — which is exactly how the official solution suggests finding it.</p><h3>The full matching</h3><p>E1 A, E2 N, E3 I, E4 T, E5 E, E6 M, E7 H, E8 L, E9 F, E10 U, E11 D, E12 J, E13 V, E14 Q, E15 W, E16 S, E17 R, E18 P, E19 O, E20 C, E21 B, E22 Y, E23 G, E24 K, E25 X.</p><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Matching puzzles in an unfamiliar script are usually solved by structure before sound: count words, find repeats, match lengths, and leave the one item that behaves differently (here, a translated name) to the very end.</p>",
